#: includes/class-tips.php:49
msgid "Some protection cannot be a switch in a plugin: it lives in your hosting, your DNS or your habits. These are the steps worth taking, strongest first in each group. Each one says what it protects against and what it does not."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: number of tips in the group.
#: includes/class-tips.php:58
#, php-format
msgid "%d tip"
msgid_plural "%d tips"
msgstr[0] ""
msgstr[1] ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:76
msgid "Does not help against:"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:96
msgid "Ten minutes in the admin area"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:97
msgid "Things any admin can do today, without hosting access."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:100
msgid "Turn on two-factor login for every admin"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:102
msgid "A second step at login, a code from an app on your phone, stops someone who guessed, bought or phished an admin password. Stolen and reused passwords are one of the most common ways into a WordPress site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:103
msgid "Free options: the Two Factor plugin, made by WordPress core contributors, or the login security part of a security plugin you already use."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:105
msgid "holes in plugins and themes: they let an attacker in without logging in at all."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:108
msgid "Keep plugins and themes updated, delete the ones you do not use"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:110
msgid "Most break-ins go through a known hole in an old plugin or theme. Turn on auto-updates for plugins you trust (the Auto-updates column on the Plugins screen); minor WordPress releases update themselves by default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:111
msgid "Delete plugins and themes you have switched off: their files stay on the server and can still be opened directly. Until you delete them, the <strong>Block PHP files of inactive plugins</strong> setting on the Advanced tab keeps them shut."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:112
msgid "Never install \"nulled\" copies of paid plugins from unofficial sites. Many of them come with a backdoor already inside."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:114
msgid "a site that is already hacked: an update replaces the plugin files, but not the backdoors an attacker left elsewhere."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:117
msgid "Keep the number of admins small"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:119
msgid "Every admin account is a full key to the site. Give people the lowest role that does the job: Editor for content, Shop manager for orders. Remove the accounts of people and agencies who no longer work on the site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:121
msgid "plugin holes that run code without a login."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:124
msgid "A different password for each admin, kept in a password manager"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:126
msgid "Passwords leaked from other sites are tried on WordPress logins every day. A password manager makes a long unique password as easy to use as a short one."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:128
msgid "phishing pages and malware on your own computer. Two-factor login covers part of that."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:131
msgid "Keep registration closed unless you need it"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:133
msgid "In Settings → General, leave \"Anyone can register\" off unless the site needs accounts, and keep \"New User Default Role\" at Subscriber or Customer. A role with more rights there gives it to everyone who signs up."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:134
msgid "The <strong>E-mail me about new administrators</strong> setting on the General tab also e-mails you when registration opens with a risky role."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:140
msgid "Hosting and DNS"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:141
msgid "Done once, in the hosting panel or at the domain registrar."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:144
msgid "Backups kept away from the server, and one test restore"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:146
msgid "A backup that lives on the same server can be deleted or encrypted together with the site. Keep copies somewhere else, for example in your host's backup service or in cloud storage, for at least a few weeks: an infection is often found weeks after it started."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:147
msgid "Restore a backup to a test copy once, so you know it works before you need it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:149
msgid "the hole that let the attacker in. After a restore, close it, or the site gets hacked again."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:152
msgid "Two-factor login for the hosting panel, SFTP or SSH instead of FTP"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:154
msgid "Whoever gets into the hosting panel or FTP gets every file and the database, and no WordPress setting can stop them. Turn on two-factor login in the hosting panel and at your domain registrar."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:155
msgid "Plain FTP sends the password unencrypted. Use SFTP or SSH, with a key instead of a password if your host allows it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:159
msgid "One site, one hosting user"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:161
msgid "When several sites run under one hosting user, a hole in one of them lets the attacker change the files of all the others. Cleaning one site and leaving the rest infected is a common reason a site gets hacked again a week later."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:162
msgid "Ask your host for a separate user, or at least a separate PHP pool, for each site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:166
msgid "A firewall in front of the site"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:168
msgid "A web application firewall at your host or a service like Cloudflare stops much of the bot traffic and known attacks before they reach WordPress, and takes load off the server."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:170
msgid "an outdated plugin. New or unusual attacks still get through, so updates still matter."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:173
msgid "A PHP version that still gets security fixes"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: PHP version of this site.
#: includes/class-tips.php:176
#, php-format
msgid "This site runs PHP %s. Versions past their end of life get no security fixes; Tools → Site Health warns about them. Your host can switch the version in the panel. Try the new one on a copy first: very old plugins can break."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:180
msgid "SPF, DKIM and DMARC records for your domain"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:182
msgid "These DNS records tell mail servers which servers may send mail for your domain. Without them, password reset e-mails and this plugin's alerts often land in spam, and anyone can send mail that looks like it comes from your domain."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:183
msgid "Your host or mail provider gives you the exact values; you add them at your DNS provider."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:185
msgid "look-alike domains. DMARC stops fake mail from your exact domain only."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:190
msgid "For developers"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:191
msgid "Server and code settings for sites you deploy and look after."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:194
msgid "Lock file changes on sites deployed with Git"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:196
msgid "When code reaches the site only through your deploy, turn off installing, updating and editing from the admin area. Then a stolen admin password alone cannot upload a plugin with a backdoor. Updates then come only through your deploy too. Add this line to wp-config.php:"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:199
msgid "holes in the plugins that are already installed."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:202
msgid "Compare files with the originals on WordPress.org"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:204
msgid "WP-CLI checks WordPress and plugin files against the copies on WordPress.org and lists every changed or extra file. On sites deployed with Git, git status does the same for your own code. Run it from cron and send yourself the output."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:207
msgid "premium plugins and themes: they are not on WordPress.org, so there is nothing to compare them with."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:210
msgid "Protect wp-config.php"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:212
msgid "It holds the database password and the secret keys. Set its permissions to 400 or 440, so only the owner can read it, or move it one folder above the site root: WordPress finds it there by itself."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:217
msgid "New secret keys after a break-in"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:219
msgid "New keys and salts in wp-config.php log everyone out, and login cookies made with the old keys stop working, even if the attacker read wp-config.php. The <strong>Log out everyone</strong> button on the If hacked tab ends all sessions and deletes application passwords, but keeps the keys. Do both, and give every admin a new password."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:224
msgid "Limit the login page by IP address or a second password"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:226
msgid "An IP allow list or HTTP Basic Auth on wp-login.php and wp-admin stops password guessing before WordPress even starts. Leave wp-admin/admin-ajax.php open: forms and plugins on the public pages use it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:228
msgid "plugin holes reached through admin-ajax.php or the REST API, which stay open to everyone."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:231
msgid "A database user with only the rights WordPress needs"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:233
msgid "A separate database user for each site keeps one hacked site out of the others' databases. Taking away more rights helps less than it seems: updates and plugin activation need CREATE and ALTER, and without them updates fail quietly."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:237
msgid "A table prefix other than wp_"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-tips.php:239
msgid "Last on the list for a reason. An SQL injection that can read your data can read the table names too, so a different prefix stops only the most basic automated attacks. Choose one when you install a new site; on a live site, change it only with a fresh backup and a tested script."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: domain name.
#: includes/class-tips.php:315
#, php-format
msgid "Check the records of %s"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: domain name.
#: includes/class-tips.php:321
#, php-format
