#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:105
msgid "E-mail me about new administrators"
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:112
msgid "When an account becomes an administrator, the site e-mail address gets a message with the user name, the e-mail of the account and who made the change. The plugin also compares the administrators in the database with the list it saw last time: after every login, on admin pages and at least once an hour while the site gets visits, without WP-Cron. So an account added straight into the database, or hidden from the Users screen, is reported too, at the latest when someone logs in with it. Hacked sites often get an extra admin with a random name like \"a3f9c2\"."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:113
msgid "Taking over an existing administrator is reported too: an e-mail goes out when the e-mail address or the password of any administrator changes, and when the site e-mail address changes (then to the old address as well, because the alerts would go to the new one)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:114
msgid "You also get an e-mail when anyone becomes able to register on the site, or when the role for new users changes while registration is open. A known attack through a plugin hole turns on \"Anyone can register\" with Administrator as the new user role, then simply registers."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:121
msgid "Nothing stops working. You also get the e-mail when you add an administrator yourself. If e-mail from this site does not arrive, the alert does not arrive either, so check that the site can send mail."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:128
msgid "Turn this off and save. No users or roles are changed."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: role name.
#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:197
#, php-format
msgid "Anyone can register on this site and gets the role \"%s\", which can change other people's content, users or the site itself. If you did not set this up on purpose, set the new user role to Subscriber or turn off \"Anyone can register\" in Settings → General."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: date.
#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:223
#, php-format
msgid "Created on %s."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: user name.
#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:228
#, php-format
msgid "The account \"%s\" has administrator rights without the Administrator role: through another role or rights added to the account itself. Malware does this so the account does not show up as an administrator. If you did not set this up, check this account."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: user name.
#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:232
#, php-format
msgid "The administrator \"%s\" is in the database but hidden from the Users screen. Something on this site filters the user list, which is how malware hides its accounts. Check this account and the must-use plugins."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: user name.
#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:235
#, php-format
msgid "The administrator \"%s\" has a name that looks random. If you do not know this account, check it."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: user name.
#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:238
#, php-format
msgid "The administrator \"%s\" was added in the last 7 days."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:450
msgid "The record of administrator accounts was changed outside the plugin"
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-admin-alert.php:452
msgid "The HelloAdmin Guard plugin keeps the e-mail addresses of the administrators and a fingerprint of their passwords, to notice when an existing administrator account is taken over. That record was changed or removed outside the plugin, for example straight in the database. The plugin cannot tell now whether an e-mail address or a password changed before that."
