msgid "The server answers 404 to any PHP file in the uploads folder, also one with a second extension (shell.php.jpg). If one of the site's plugins or forms has a hole in file uploads and someone puts a PHP file there, it cannot be opened from the browser."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-no-php-in-uploads.php:55
msgid "If a plugin on the site keeps its own PHP scripts in the uploads folder and opens them from the browser, it stops working. Such plugins are rare today. Images, PDFs and other uploads keep working."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-old-plugins.php:60
msgid "E-mail me about abandoned and closed plugins"
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-old-plugins.php:67
msgid "A plugin nobody maintains gets no fixes when a hole is found in it. Twice a day WordPress asks WordPress.org about updates for your plugins. From that same answer this setting finds plugins that were not tested with the last 3 major WordPress releases, and plugins WordPress.org no longer answers for: they were closed there, often because of a security problem, or they came from somewhere else. They are listed on this card. When you turn this on, the administration address (Settings → General) gets one e-mail with the plugins found right now, and later an e-mail each time a new one shows up. The plugin makes no requests of its own for this."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-old-plugins.php:74
msgid "Nothing stops working; the plugins are only listed. Premium and custom plugins that do not say where their updates come from (the Update URI header) show up in the second list too: check where you got them and whether they still get updates."
msgstr ""

#: includes/features/class-old-plugins.php:81
